<span class="ltx_personname">Christopher R.S. Banerji
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Comprehensive Cancer Centre, Guy’s Hospital, King’s College London, Great Maze Pond, London, SE1 9RT, UK
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>The Alan Turing Institute, NW1 2DB, UK
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Corresponding author: christopher.banerji@kcl.ac.uk
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">March 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Aligning human-interpretable concepts with the internal representations learned by modern machine learning systems remains a central challenge for interpretable AI. We introduce a geometric framework for comparing supervised human concepts with unsupervised intermediate representations extracted from foundation model embeddings. Motivated by the role of conceptual leaps in scientific discovery, we formalise the notion of concept frustration: a contradiction that arises when an unobserved concept induces relationships between known concepts that cannot be made consistent within an existing ontology. We develop task-aligned similarity measures that detect concept frustration between supervised concept-based models and unsupervised representations derived from foundation models, and show that the phenomenon is detectable in task-aligned geometry while conventional Euclidean comparisons fail. Under a linear–Gaussian generative model we derive a closed-form expression for Bayes-optimal concept-based classifier accuracy, decomposing predictive signal into known–known, known–unknown and unknown–unknown contributions and identifying analytically where frustration affects performance. Experiments on synthetic data and real language and vision tasks demonstrate that frustration can be detected in foundation model representations and that incorporating a frustrating concept into an interpretable model reorganises the geometry of learned concept representations, to better align human and machine reasoning. These results suggest a principled framework for diagnosing incomplete concept ontologies and aligning human and machine conceptual reasoning, with implications for the development and validation of safe interpretable AI for high-risk applications.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human-level interpretability in artificial intelligence (AI) is critical for high-consequence domains such as medicine, scientific discovery and criminal justice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. In such settings, errors can have catastrophic consequences and accountability, as well as emerging regulation, requires not only accurate predictions but transparent reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There are two prominent strategies for securing transparency. (1) Post-hoc feature attribution, where we train a black box model, then perform techniques such as SHAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, LIME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> or Integrated Gradients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> to identify task-relevant elements of the input. Similarly, mechanistic interpretability decomposes feature representations of trained models using methods such as sparse autoencoders (SAEs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. (2) Interpretability-by-design, where we optimise aspects of the model architecture, training regime or input set, for interpretability pre-hoc. This includes the concept bottleneck model (CBM), where we constrain the model to learn a set of supervised concepts that are pre-defined by human domain experts and therefore guaranteed to be interpretable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Both strategies have limitations. Since post-hoc methods construct local surrogates of a complex black-box model, there is no guarantee they will provide accurate or holistic interpretations of model behaviour <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, conversely SAE derived features often exhibit polysemanticity and feature superposition, degrading interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. In CBMs, the bottleneck constraint that ensures interpretability also limits model expressivity, undermining performance when task-relevant features are excluded from the supervised concepts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">There are at least two solutions to this problem in CBMs. The first is to allow predictive information to flow through an auxiliary pathway that bypasses the concept bottleneck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. These “concept side-channel models” increase expressivity but at the expense of interpretability. The second is to optimise the concept set - or <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ontology</em> - for the task at hand. However, constructing an adequate ontology first requires a reliable diagnostic for cases when the current ontology is inadequate. Recent work has addressed this by comparing supervised CBM concepts with unsupervised SAE features through geometric alignment in Euclidean space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. While foundational, such approaches rely on restrictive assumptions: that supervised concepts are statistically independent, that alignment should be assessed in a task-agnostic geometry and that particular parameter constraints (such as non-negativity of concept codes) are preserved. These assumptions are often violated in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Here we introduce a diagnostic framework for incomplete concept ontologies that addresses limitations of prior work. We identify a phenomenon we term <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concept frustration</em>, in which information that is predictive of the task but absent from the supervised concept set induces contradictions between known concepts, revealing that the ontology is incomplete. We show that detecting frustration requires a task-aligned geometry, rather than a Euclidean geometry, and that frustration degrades both performance and interpretability in CBMs. In real-world language and vision tasks, resolving frustration by incorporating a previously unobserved frustrating concept reorganises existing concept geometry, restoring consistency between supervised and unsupervised concept sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these results provide a pathway for identifying hidden conceptual dimensions, that can fundamentally reorganise how supervised models, and even human overseers, reason about the world.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Flatworld: a motivating example</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate frustration, consider a thought experiment: a flatworlder navigates the Earth using two trusted concepts: distance to the North Pole and distance to the South Pole. They expect these concepts to oppose each other, so that moving closer to one Pole moves them farther from the other. However, due to the Earth’s curvature, a traveller who moves closer to the Earth’s centre, say by diving in an ocean, can move closer to both Poles simultaneously (Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.1 Flatworld: a motivating example ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This introduces an unknown concept, depth, that is not part of the flatworlder’s ontology but distorts how known concepts relate. We refer to such a hidden, task-relevant concept as <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">frustrating</em>: without it, the flatworlder cannot form a coherent picture of their world.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29654v1/Figure_2.2.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/289;" width="476" height="289" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S2.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The importance of concept frustration.</span> Consider a flatworlder training a model to predict a position on Earth from a complex signal (such as from a satellite), in an interpretable manner. They suggest two known concepts as <math id="S2.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>=distance form the North Pole and <math id="S2.F1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>=distance from the South pole. They expect these to be anti-correlated. Their trained model is highly accurate of position, but reasons using an unknown concept <math id="S2.F1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, which frustrates their known inter-concept semantics. Increasing <math id="S2.F1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> forces <math id="S2.F1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to be positively correlated. Once <math id="S2.F1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is understood as distance to the core of a curved Earth, the frustration makes sense and known understanding of the system is improved.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Background and models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> denote a space of input data. An arbitrary feature extractor (e.g., a foundation model) maps inputs to an activation space <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:X\to A\subset\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:X\to A\subset\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}=f(\mathbf{x})\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}=f(\mathbf{x})\in A</annotation></semantics></math> for its activation vector (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Background and models ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>A). We treat <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> as a dense representation of features relevant for predicting downstream tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> denote an outcome variable and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}C_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}C_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> a set of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> concepts. We assume that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are associated, such that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(Y|C)\not=p(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(Y|C)\not=p(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly we assume that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are associated, i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(C|A)\not=p(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(C|A)\not=p(C)</annotation></semantics></math>, which naturally implies an association between <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(Y|A)\not=p(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(Y|A)\not=p(Y)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We partition <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\text{known}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\text{known}}</annotation></semantics></math> known concepts,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{known}}=\bigcup_{j=1}^{k_{\text{known}}}C_{j}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub></munderover><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{known}}=\bigcup_{j=1}^{k_{\text{known}}}C_{j},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-k_{\text{known}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-k_{\text{known}}</annotation></semantics></math> unknown concepts,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{unknown}}=\bigcup_{j=k_{\text{known}}+1}^{k}C_{j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>unknown</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{unknown}}=\bigcup_{j=k_{\text{known}}+1}^{k}C_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}\in A</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{known}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{known}}</annotation></semantics></math> is observed, while <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{unknown}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>unknown</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{unknown}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A common goal of interpretable modelling is to predict <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> via intermediate concepts <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, enabling human-understandable reasoning (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Background and models ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>B). We consider three modelling strategies. Further architectural and training details are provided in the Methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Black-box model.</span>
We directly approximate <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(Y\mid A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(Y\mid A)</annotation></semantics></math> using a model trained on <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{a}_{i},y_{i})_{i=1}^{N_{\text{train}}}\subset(A,Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{a}_{i},y_{i})_{i=1}^{N_{\text{train}}}\subset(A,Y)</annotation></semantics></math>. In our implementation, we use a single hidden layer of width <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> with ReLU activation and consider <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> as a binary task (extension to non-binary is straightforward). The hidden layer outputs logits <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math>, from which the predicted task label is obtained via a sigmoid transformation, <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
This approach can achieve high predictive accuracy, but offers no direct interpretability (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Background and models ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>C). See <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Model architectures ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> for further details.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sparse autoencoder (SAE).</span>
We extract <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\text{SAE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\text{SAE}}</annotation></semantics></math> unsupervised latent features from <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, using a model trained on <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{a}_{i})_{i=1}^{N_{\text{train}}}\subset A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{a}_{i})_{i=1}^{N_{\text{train}}}\subset A</annotation></semantics></math>. An encoder <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s:\mathbb{R}^{r}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s:\mathbb{R}^{r}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}}</annotation></semantics></math> maps activations to the latent feature space</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(\mathbf{a})=\mathrm{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{SAE}}\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{SAE}})\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(\mathbf{a})=\mathrm{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{\text{SAE}}\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}_{\text{SAE}})\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{\text{SAE}}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{\text{SAE}}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned matrix of weights and <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{\text{SAE}}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{\text{SAE}}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned vector of biases, and a linear decoder reconstructs</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{a}}=s(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{D}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐃</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{a}}=s(\mathbf{a})\mathbf{D},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dictionary whose rows define atoms in activation space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The SAE decomposes <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> into unsupervised features and may loosely estimate <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(C|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(C|A)</annotation></semantics></math>, however these features are not conditioned on relevance to the task <math id="S2.I1.i2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Background and models ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>D).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concept bottleneck model (CBM).</span> Known concepts are first predicted from activations, and these estimated concepts are subsequently used to predict the outcome. We consider here joint training from <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{a}_{i},\mathbf{c}_{i}^{known},y_{i})_{i=1}^{N_{\text{train}}}\subset(A,C_{known},Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>train</mtext></msub></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{a}_{i},\mathbf{c}_{i}^{known},y_{i})_{i=1}^{N_{\text{train}}}\subset(A,C_{known},Y)</annotation></semantics></math>, and assume <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a sufficiently processed representation that concepts are linearly deducible. The CBM thus learns a linear concept mapping</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\text{known}}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{a}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐐𝐚</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\text{known}}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{a},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{known}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{known}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, together with a linear task head that maps the predicted concepts to a binary outcome via a sigmoid transformation, yielding <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The CBM estimates <math id="S2.I1.i3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(C_{\text{known}}\mid A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(C_{\text{known}}\mid A)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(Y\mid\hat{C}_{\text{known}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(Y\mid\hat{C}_{\text{known}})</annotation></semantics></math>. When the task depends strongly on <math id="S2.I1.i3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{unknown}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>unknown</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{unknown}}</annotation></semantics></math>, predictive accuracy may degrade relative to the black-box model. However, the CBM provides direct interpretability by expressing predictions as linear functions of supervised concepts (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.2 Background and models ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>E).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29654v1/Figure_1.2.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/268;" width="476" height="268"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of approach.</span> (A) Raw data is typically processed by a feature extractor (such as a foundation model) to produce an activation vector. (B) Activations can be used to predict an outcome reasoned on a set of intermediate concepts some which may be known prior to predicting the task. This task can be approached using (C) a black box model, (D) a sparse autoencoder (E) a concept bottleneck model. </figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>A task-aligned geometry to compare supervised and unsupervised concepts</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The three models provide three outputs:</p>
<ol id="S2.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Black box model: An estimate of <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(Y|A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(Y|A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SAE: A matrix <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{SAE}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, describing a dictionary of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\text{SAE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\text{SAE}}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unsupervised</em> concepts extracted from <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, represented as <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vectors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CBM: A matrix <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{known}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{known}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> describing a dictionary of <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{known}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{known}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">supervised</em> concepts extracted from <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, represented as <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vectors.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We wish to compare the known concepts in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> to the unsupervised concepts in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The simplest way is using Euclidean geometry over <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, defining a standard inner product as:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle\mathbf{q}_{i},\mathbf{d}_{j}\rangle_{E}=\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{d}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle\mathbf{q}_{i},\mathbf{d}_{j}\rangle_{E}=\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{d}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are the <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>j</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> rows of <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, this measures alignment of the SAE and CBM derived concepts in Euclidean space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">However, this geometry is not anchored to the predictive task. Many SAE features may be irrelevant to <math id="S2.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, and Euclidean alignment need not reflect task-relevant relationships. Consequently, Euclidean similarity does not guarantee semantically meaningful concept comparisons with respect to the outcome.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A more natural geometry is that of the statistical manifold <math id="S2.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(A,p(y|\mathbf{a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(A,p(y|\mathbf{a}))</annotation></semantics></math>, equipped with the Fisher information metric</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{a})}\left[\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})^{\top}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{a})}\left[\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})^{\top}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Under our single-layer black-box model, this metric admits a closed-form expression.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Closed-form Fisher metric for one-hidden-layer binary model)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the black-box model defined above with sigmoid output. The Fisher information metric on activation space satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=p(\mathbf{a})(1-p(\mathbf{a}))\,g(\mathbf{a})g(\mathbf{a})^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=p(\mathbf{a})(1-p(\mathbf{a}))\,g(\mathbf{a})g(\mathbf{a})^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{a})\equiv p(y=1|\mathbf{a})=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{a})\equiv p(y=1|\mathbf{a})=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{a})=\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{a})=\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The proof and explicit form of <math id="S2.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math> are provided in the Methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For fixed <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{A}(\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{A}(\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math> is a rank-one positive semi-definite matrix, as it is the outer product of <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math> with itself. Thus the pointwise Fisher metric captures sensitivity only along a single direction in activation space. Moreover,
whenever <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{a})\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{a})\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> the prefactor vanishes and <math id="S2.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently, the Fisher geometry is expected to be more informative near the decision boundary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We therefore define a global, task-aligned quadratic form by averaging over activations near the decision boundary. Let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\mathrm{ave}}:=\{\mathbf{a}\in A_{\mathrm{train}}:p(\mathbf{a})\in[p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}}]\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>train</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>high</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\mathrm{ave}}:=\{\mathbf{a}\in A_{\mathrm{train}}:p(\mathbf{a})\in[p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}}]\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">We define</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{A}=\frac{1}{|A_{\mathrm{ave}}|}\sum_{\mathbf{a}\in A_{\mathrm{ave}}}p(\mathbf{a})(1-p(\mathbf{a}))\,g(\mathbf{a})g(\mathbf{a})^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>ave</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{A}=\frac{1}{|A_{\mathrm{ave}}|}\sum_{\mathbf{a}\in A_{\mathrm{ave}}}p(\mathbf{a})(1-p(\mathbf{a}))\,g(\mathbf{a})g(\mathbf{a})^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Equipped with (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.3 A task-aligned geometry to compare supervised and unsupervised concepts ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), we define the task-aligned inner product</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\langle\mathbf{q}_{i},\mathbf{d}_{j}\rangle_{F_{A}}=\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\bar{F}_{A}\mathbf{d}_{j}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\langle\mathbf{q}_{i},\mathbf{d}_{j}\rangle_{F_{A}}=\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\bar{F}_{A}\mathbf{d}_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p10.2" class="ltx_p">This inner product will detect alignment between SAE and CBM derived concepts in a geometry aligned to task prediction and can thus be expected uncover more task-relevant inter-concept semantics than a Euclidean geometry.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Defining and measuring concept frustration</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now formalise a framework to compare supervised and unsupervised concept directions under <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define two concept similarity matrices. First, the task aligned similarity between known and unsupervised concepts <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> with elements:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{F_{A}}_{ij}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\bar{F}_{A}\mathbf{d}_{j}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{i}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}\,\|\mathbf{d}_{j}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{F_{A}}_{ij}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\bar{F}_{A}\mathbf{d}_{j}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{i}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}\,\|\mathbf{d}_{j}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{v}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}:=\sqrt{\mathbf{v}^{\top}\bar{F}_{A}\mathbf{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐯</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{v}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}:=\sqrt{\mathbf{v}^{\top}\bar{F}_{A}\mathbf{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{F_{A}}_{ij}\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{F_{A}}_{ij}\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> measures alignment between <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{d}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{d}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in the task-aligned geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, the task-aligned similarities between supervised concepts <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}^{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}^{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> with elements:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{F_{A}}_{rl}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{r}^{\top}\bar{F}_{A}\mathbf{q}_{l}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{r}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}\,\|\mathbf{q}_{l}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{F_{A}}_{rl}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{r}^{\top}\bar{F}_{A}\mathbf{q}_{l}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{r}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}\,\|\mathbf{q}_{l}\|_{\bar{F}_{A}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z^{F_{A}}_{rl}\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^{F_{A}}_{rl}\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the task-aligned relation between known concepts <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{q}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{q}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison, we define task-agnostic Euclidean similarities:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{E}_{ij}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{d}_{j}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{i}\|_{2}\,\|\mathbf{d}_{j}\|_{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{E}_{ij}=\frac{\mathbf{q}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{d}_{j}}{\|\mathbf{q}_{i}\|_{2}\,\|\mathbf{d}_{j}\|_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> defined analogously for supervised concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We now formally define concept frustration and related quantities:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Concept triplet frustration)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\neq l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\neq l</annotation></semantics></math> index two known concepts and let <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> index an unsupervised direction.
The triplet <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{q}_{r},\mathbf{q}_{l},\mathbf{d}_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐪</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐝</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{q}_{r},\mathbf{q}_{l},\mathbf{d}_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits concept frustration if</span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sign}\big(Z^{F_{A}}_{rl}\big)\neq\mathrm{sign}\big(S^{F_{A}}_{rj}S^{F_{A}}_{lj}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sign}\big(Z^{F_{A}}_{rl}\big)\neq\mathrm{sign}\big(S^{F_{A}}_{rj}S^{F_{A}}_{lj}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">This definition mirrors frustration in spin systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, where spins are discrete random variables with covariances determined by pairwise couplings <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. A triangle of sites <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r,l,j\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r,l,j\}</annotation></semantics></math> is frustrated when the three pairwise couplings <math id="S2.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{rl},J_{lj},J_{jr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{rl},J_{lj},J_{jr}</annotation></semantics></math> between those sites cannot be satisfied simultaneously. This occurs when</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sign}(J_{rl}J_{lj}J_{jr})=-1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sign}(J_{rl}J_{lj}J_{jr})=-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">which is equivalent to equation (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In Definition 1 (Concept triplet frustration). ‣ 2.4 Defining and measuring concept frustration ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Maximally frustrating direction)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For each known concept pair <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,l)</annotation></semantics></math>, define the maximally frustrating unsupervised direction <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j^{*}(r,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{*}(r,l)</annotation></semantics></math> via</span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="j^{*}(r,l)=\arg\max_{j:\mathrm{sign}(Z^{F_{A}}_{rl})\neq\mathrm{sign}(S^{F_{A}}_{rj}S^{F_{A}}_{lj})}\big|S^{F_{A}}_{rj}S^{F_{A}}_{lj}\big|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>arg</mi><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j^{*}(r,l)=\arg\max_{j:\mathrm{sign}(Z^{F_{A}}_{rl})\neq\mathrm{sign}(S^{F_{A}}_{rj}S^{F_{A}}_{lj})}\big|S^{F_{A}}_{rj}S^{F_{A}}_{lj}\big|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If no such <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> exists, the known concept pair is unfrustrated.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Pairwise frustration)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define the pairwise frustration for a known concept pair <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(r,l)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(r,l)</annotation></semantics></math> as the magnitude of the maximally frustrating direction:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(r,l)=\begin{cases}\big|S^{F_{A}}_{rj^{*}}S^{F_{A}}_{lj^{*}}\big|&amp;\text{if }j^{*}(r,l)\text{ exists},\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Frust</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"> exists</mtext></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(r,l)=\begin{cases}\big|S^{F_{A}}_{rj^{*}}S^{F_{A}}_{lj^{*}}\big|&amp;\text{if }j^{*}(r,l)\text{ exists},\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Global frustration)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define the global frustration as the average pairwise frustration across all known concept pairs:</span></p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{F_{A}}=\frac{2}{k_{\mathrm{known}}(k_{\mathrm{known}}-1)}\sum_{l&lt;r}\mathrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(r,l)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>Frust</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{F_{A}}=\frac{2}{k_{\mathrm{known}}(k_{\mathrm{known}}-1)}\sum_{l&lt;r}\mathrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(r,l).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{F_{A}}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{F_{A}}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> measures the average magnitude of task-aligned geometric contradiction between supervised concepts mediated by unsupervised directions.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We note that via Euclidean similarities <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{S}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐒</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{S}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Z}^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐙</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Z}^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, Definitions 1-4 can be restated in Euclidean geometry yielding the Euclidean global frustration <math id="S2.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, for comparison.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Detecting concept frustration in the flatworld</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a motivating example, we return to our flatworlder thought experiment. Consider three concepts <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2},C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2},C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> predictive of an outcome <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, where only <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are supervised. Let <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denote surface-parallel distance to the North and South poles respectively. For a traveler constrained to the Earth’s surface, moving closer to the North pole necessarily moves them farther from the South pole, so the two distances behave as opposing concepts, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(C_{1},C_{2})&lt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(C_{1},C_{2})&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now allow the traveler to descend below the surface. In a spherical (round-world) geometry of radius 1, depth changes the surface geometry. Defining depth of a point as its inward distance from the surface, a traveler at depth <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> lies on a sphere of radius <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-d</annotation></semantics></math>, and the supervised concepts are defined as geodesic distances on that sphere to the North and South poles (Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.5 Detecting concept frustration in the flatworld ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>A). Increasing depth therefore shortens both geodesic distances simultaneously. We denote by <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> depth below the surface and assume this concept is unsupervised. On a round Earth this induces</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(C_{1},C_{3})&lt;0,\qquad\mathrm{Cov}(C_{2},C_{3})&lt;0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(C_{1},C_{3})&lt;0,\qquad\mathrm{Cov}(C_{2},C_{3})&lt;0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Together with <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(C_{1},C_{2})&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(C_{1},C_{2})&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, this sign pattern corresponds to the triplet-frustration structure defined in the previous section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, under a cylindrical (flatworld) geometry, depth corresponds to translation orthogonal to a flat disk. The supervised concepts are defined as planar distances within the disk at fixed depth. In this case, depth does not alter surface geometry and is independent of the planar distances, so</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(C_{1},C_{3})=0,\qquad\mathrm{Cov}(C_{2},C_{3})=0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(C_{1},C_{3})=0,\qquad\mathrm{Cov}(C_{2},C_{3})=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and no covariance-based concept frustration is expected.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This motivates a simple “treasure hunter” task in which we determine whether a treasure is within a radius of our location from a signal encoding its position, using an interpretable model with known concepts <math id="S2.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, an activation <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes a 3D location <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and the goal is to predict whether <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math> lies within distance <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> of a fixed reference point <math id="S2.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}=(1,0,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}=(1,0,0)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y(\mathbf{p})=\mathds{1}\{\|\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{e}\|_{2}&lt;R\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\mathbf{p})=\mathds{1}\{\|\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{e}\|_{2}&lt;R\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We compare two geometries: cylindrical and spherical. In both cases we define the same supervised concepts abstractly as surface-parallel distances to the North and South poles,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}(\mathbf{p})=\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{surface}}(\mathbf{p},\mathrm{North}),\qquad C_{2}(\mathbf{p})=\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{surface}}(\mathbf{p},\mathrm{South})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>surface</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>North</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>dist</mi><mi>surface</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>South</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}(\mathbf{p})=\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{surface}}(\mathbf{p},\mathrm{North}),\qquad C_{2}(\mathbf{p})=\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{surface}}(\mathbf{p},\mathrm{South}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p6.2" class="ltx_p">with depth <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> treated as unknown. We consider a linear-Gaussian mapping from concepts to activations to generate the signal <math id="S2.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math>. Details are provided in the Methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29654v1/fig_3_globe_paired.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/484;" width="476" height="484" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The treasure hunter task and frustration.</span> A. Consider that we receive a complex signal encoding the position of a treasure, from this signal we wish to predict the binary task of identifying whether a treasure is in a fixed search radius of our position. We employ an interpretable model where known concepts <math id="S2.F3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are the distance to the North and South polar lines parallel to the Earth’s surface, and we consider the unknown concept <math id="S2.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> to denote depth below the surface. We consider two geometries: a flat Earth in which there is no frustration between known and unknown concepts, and a round Earth in which there is frustration. B. Box and violin plots display <math id="S2.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> calculated from 50 simulations of the flat and round Earth geometry treasure hunter tasks, alongside paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values and 95% confidence intervals of the median difference between round and flat Earth metric values. We see that <math id="S2.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> detects frustration while <math id="S2.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> does not.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Across 50 simulations per geometry, we trained the black-box model, SAE, and CBM and computed the frustration metrics defined above. Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.5 Detecting concept frustration in the flatworld ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>B shows that the task-aligned frustration score <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is significantly higher in the spherical setting than in the cylindrical (paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=5.4\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>5.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=5.4\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>), consistent with stronger task-aligned concept frustration in the round-world setting. In contrast, the Euclidean analogue <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> does not distinguish the two settings (paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.SS5.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.118" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.118</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.118</annotation></semantics></math>). This indicates that frustration is not detectable from ambient geometric alignment alone, but emerges when concept directions are evaluated in a geometry weighted by their contribution to task uncertainty. Further comparisons are provided in Supplementary Table S1.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6 </span>A data model to control concept frustration</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To study concept frustration under controlled conditions, we construct a synthetic data generator in which the covariance structure of supervised and unsupervised concepts can be manipulated explicitly while preserving the marginal structure of known concepts (see Methods for details).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a concept vector</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}=(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u})\in\mathbb{R}^{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝌</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}=(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u})\in\mathbb{R}^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{known}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{known}}</annotation></semantics></math> supervised concepts and <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{unknown}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{unknown}}</annotation></semantics></math> unsupervised concepts. Concepts are sampled from a zero-mean Gaussian distribution with covariance</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}(\alpha)=\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{B}_{kk}&amp;\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\\
\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)&amp;\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}(\alpha)=\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{B}_{kk}&amp;\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\\
\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)&amp;\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{kk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{kk}</annotation></semantics></math> is the covariance among known concepts and <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> the cross-covariance between known and unknown concepts. The parameter <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in[-1,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in[-1,1]</annotation></semantics></math> enforces three regimes. When <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, known and unknown concepts are independent. For <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, cross-block correlations reinforce the correlation structure within <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{kk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{kk}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing a non-frustrated regime. For <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, they oppose it, generating concept frustration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is positive semi-definite, we take <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> to be of the form</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)=\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}+\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>temp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)=\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}+\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>temp</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}</annotation></semantics></math> an <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-independent baseline covariance capturing variation intrinsic to the unsupervised concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We define the task via</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}+\eta,\qquad\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}+\eta,\qquad\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}=(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝍</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}=(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})</annotation></semantics></math> specifies the task-relevant directions on known and unknown concepts, and <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> controls label noise. Binary labels are then generated as
<math id="S2.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y=\mathds{1}\{\tau&gt;0\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\mathds{1}\{\tau&gt;0\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
A separate parameter <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> controls how strongly the task depends on known versus unknown concepts through the construction of <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>. Activations are generated from concepts through a linear-Gaussian map.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This provides a controlled environment in which (i) the supervised concept ontology remains fixed across regimes, (ii) frustration strength is governed solely by <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and (iii) the task dependence on unknown structure is governed by <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.7 </span>Concept-optimal accuracy can be interpreted in terms of frustration</h3>

<div id="S2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider the concept-optimal Bayes classifier for the synthetic data defined in Section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 A data model to control concept frustration ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, that minimises the expected error of predicting the task, given knowledge of supervised concepts. We find that the accuracy of this classifier admits a closed form expression.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Concept-optimal Accuracy in the Linear–Gaussian Model)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the synthetic model defined in Section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 A data model to control concept frustration ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the concept-optimal Bayes classifier using only the known concepts <math id="Thmtheorem2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛘</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which minimises the risk</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R(\hat{y})=\mathbb{E}_{(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},y)}\left[\mathds{1}\{\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})\neq y\}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\hat{y})=\mathbb{E}_{(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},y)}\left[\mathds{1}\{\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})\neq y\}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})=\mathds{1}\{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}&gt;0\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})=\mathds{1}\{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}&gt;0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and its classification accuracy is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha)=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\left(\sqrt{\frac{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)}{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Acc</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">CBM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha)=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\left(\sqrt{\frac{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)}{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of this theorem is provided in the Methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The denominator term <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of <math id="S2.SS7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, thus</p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha_{1})&gt;\mathrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha_{2})\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha_{1})^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha_{1})&gt;\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha_{2})^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Acc</mi><mtext>CBM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>Acc</mi><mtext>CBM</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟺</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha_{1})&gt;\mathrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha_{2})\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha_{1})^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha_{1})&gt;\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha_{2})^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Expanding this term yields</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}\rangle_{E}+2\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}\rangle_{E}+\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u},\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}\rangle_{E}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}\rangle_{E}+2\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}\rangle_{E}+\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u},\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}\rangle_{E}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=T_{1}+2T_{2}+T_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=T_{1}+2T_{2}+T_{3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.2" class="ltx_p">This admits a geometric interpretation of optimal accuracy.</p>
<ol id="S2.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Supervised signal:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}:=\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}\rangle_{E}=||\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}||^{2}_{\mathbf{B}_{kk}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}:=\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}\rangle_{E}=||\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}||^{2}_{\mathbf{B}_{kk}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I3.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This measures task-relevant signal present in known concepts alone. Geometrically, it is the squared length of the supervised task direction <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in the covariance geometry induced by <math id="S2.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{kk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{kk}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This term is invariant under frustration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Cross-alignment between supervised and unknown signal:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}(\alpha):=\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}\rangle_{E}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}(\alpha):=\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}\rangle_{E}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This term captures how the unknown task direction <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> projects into known-concept space through the cross-covariance matrix <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, and whether this projection reinforces or interferes with the supervised task direction <math id="S2.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Positive alignment increases accuracy, whereas negative alignment reduces it.
Frustration acts primarily by modifying this term.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S2.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recoverable unknown signal:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{3}(\alpha):=\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u},\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}\rangle_{E}=||\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}||^{2}_{\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{3}(\alpha):=\langle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u},\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}\rangle_{E}=||\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}||^{2}_{\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.I3.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This term quantifies the magnitude of unknown task-relevant variation that becomes linearly recoverable from known concepts via cross-block correlations.
It measures signal strength rather than directional alignment, and is zero if known and unknown concepts are independent (<math id="S2.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The term</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=||\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}||_{\mathbf{B}_{temp}}^{2}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=||\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}||_{\mathbf{B}_{temp}}^{2}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=T_{4}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=T_{4}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS7.p5.2" class="ltx_p">also admits a geometric interpretation: <math id="S2.SS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is the unknown signal not recoverable from supervised concepts, and the larger this term is the worse concept-optimal accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Informally, <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> describes known-knowns, <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> known-unknowns and <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> unknown-unknowns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.8 </span>Frustration between known and unknown concepts limits CBM task accuracy</h3>

<div id="S2.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We trained CBMs, black-box models, and SAEs across 60 parameter regimes of our synthetic data generator (see Methods). Each regime was repeated across 10 independent seeds, yielding 600 matched datasets. Full results are provided in Supplementary Table S2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Frustration significantly reduced CBM task accuracy. CBM accuracy was significantly lower under <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=+1</annotation></semantics></math> compared to both <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> (paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>; Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.8 Frustration between known and unknown concepts limits CBM task accuracy ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>A). In contrast, black-box model accuracy did not differ between <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math> (paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.13</annotation></semantics></math>, Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.8 Frustration between known and unknown concepts limits CBM task accuracy ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>B), and was slightly worse when <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that frustration limits CBM performance without increasing intrinsic task difficulty.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29654v1/Figure_acc_interpret.png" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:448/423;" width="448" height="423" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S2.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CBM accuracy and interpretability are degraded under frustration.</span> Box and violin plots display (A) accuracy of black box classifiers (B) accuracy of CBM classifiers (C) concept mean-square error (MSE) for CBM classifiers and (D) concept semantic fidelity metric <math id="S2.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, calculated from 600 simulations of our synthetic data generator across a range of parameter regimes, alongside paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values and 95% confidence intervals of the median difference between <math id="S2.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> values. We see that frustration significantly degrades accuracy and interpretability of CBMs without making the task more difficult for a non-interpretable model</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.9 </span>Frustration impairs semantic interpretability of CBMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next examined the effect of frustration on concept-level interpretability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Frustration significantly impaired concept prediction accuracy. Concept mean squared error (MSE) was significantly higher under <math id="S2.SS9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=+1</annotation></semantics></math> compared to both <math id="S2.SS9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS9.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> (paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.SS9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>; Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.8 Frustration between known and unknown concepts limits CBM task accuracy ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>C).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When trained on the synthetic data, a CBM learns concepts <math id="S2.SS9.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\mathrm{known}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>known</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\mathrm{known}}</annotation></semantics></math> with covariance <math id="S2.SS9.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\mathrm{known}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>known</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\mathrm{known}})</annotation></semantics></math> that may differ from the ground-truth covariance structure <math id="S2.SS9.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}</annotation></semantics></math>. To quantify this decrease in interpretability, we define the following score.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 5</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Semantic fidelity)<span id="Thmdefinition5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The semantic fidelity score <math id="Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> measures deviation between the covariance of predicted known concepts and the ground-truth covariance structure</span></p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\frac{\|\mathrm{Cov}(\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\mathrm{known}})-\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}\|_{\mathrm{Frob}}}{\|\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}\|_{\mathrm{Frob}}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>known</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>Frob</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>Frob</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\frac{\|\mathrm{Cov}(\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\mathrm{known}})-\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}\|_{\mathrm{Frob}}}{\|\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}\|_{\mathrm{Frob}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS9.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Under frustration, <math id="S2.SS9.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> increased markedly (paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.SS9.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.8 Frustration between known and unknown concepts limits CBM task accuracy ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>D), indicating distortion of the learned inter-concept relationships.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS9.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, frustration degrades both predictive and structural interpretability of CBMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS10" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.10 </span>A task-aligned geometry detects known-unknown concept frustration</h3>

<div id="S2.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next evaluated whether frustration can be detected via our metric.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS10.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The task-aligned frustration metric <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> was significantly higher under <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=+1</annotation></semantics></math> compared to both <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math> (paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>; Figure <a href="#S2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 2.10 A task-aligned geometry detects known-unknown concept frustration ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>A). In contrast, the Euclidean analogue <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> did not reliably distinguish frustration (Figure <a href="#S2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 2.10 A task-aligned geometry detects known-unknown concept frustration ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>A). Moreover, <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> was elevated when the theoretical cross alignment term <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> was non-zero (paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas <math id="S2.SS10.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> was not (Figure <a href="#S2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 2.10 A task-aligned geometry detects known-unknown concept frustration ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>B).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS10.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, frustration is detectable in task-aligned Fisher geometry but not in Euclidean geometry.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29654v1/Figure_4_frustration_simulation.png" id="S2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/450;" width="476" height="450" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><math id="S2.F5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> detects frustration in inter-concept covariance and concept task weights between known and unknown concepts</span> Box and violin plots display <math id="S2.F5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> calculated from 600 simulations of our synthetic data model across 60 parameter regimes separated by (A) <math id="S2.F5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and (B) the sign of <math id="S2.F5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> the theoretical cross alignment term between known and unknown signal. Paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.F5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values and 95% confidence intervals of the median difference between metric values across various parameter groups are displayed. We see that <math id="S2.F5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is elevated when concept covariance between known and unknown concepts is frustrated, while <math id="S2.F5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is not. Similarly we see that <math id="S2.F5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is consistently elevated when the cross alignment term <math id="S2.F5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is non-zero, while <math id="S2.F5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is not.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.11 </span>Resolving concept frustration in real-world language and vision tasks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next evaluated whether concept frustration can be detected in real-world language and vision tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS11.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For language we considered sarcasm detection. We used a dataset of 28,000 news headlines labelled with sarcasm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and embedded them using the pretrained DeBERTa-v3-base language model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> (Methods). From a pretrained RoBERTa sentiment classifier <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> we derived three sentiment-related concepts: <math id="S2.SS11.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (positive sentiment), <math id="S2.SS11.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (negative sentiment), and <math id="S2.SS11.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (sentiment intensity). In this dataset, <math id="S2.SS11.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS11.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are inversely correlated, while <math id="S2.SS11.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is positively correlated with both, forming a frustrating triple. A linear model confirmed all three concepts independently associated with sarcasm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS11.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS11.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For vision we considered a binary classification task distinguishing gulls from terns in the CUB-200-2011 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Images were embedded using the pretrained CLIP ViT-B/16 vision encoder <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. From the CUB attribute annotations we defined three concepts: <math id="S2.SS11.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (white upperparts), <math id="S2.SS11.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (gull-like shape), and <math id="S2.SS11.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (solid wing pattern). Restricting to gull and tern species, <math id="S2.SS11.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS11.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> were positively correlated, while <math id="S2.SS11.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> was negatively correlated with <math id="S2.SS11.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and positively correlated with <math id="S2.SS11.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, forming a frustrating triple. All three concepts were independently associated with the classification outcome.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS11.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS11.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For each task we trained two CBMs on the same embedding space. CBM1 used <math id="S2.SS11.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> as supervised concepts, treating <math id="S2.SS11.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> as unknown. CBM2 used all three concepts <math id="S2.SS11.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2},C_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2},C_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> as supervised. Both models were compared against the same SAE dictionary <math id="S2.SS11.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{D}</annotation></semantics></math> learned from the embedding space. Frustration was computed only on the concept pair <math id="S2.SS11.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> for both models as <math id="S2.SS11.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frust}^{F_{A}/E}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Frust</mi><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frust}^{F_{A}/E}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math> .</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS11.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS11.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Across both tasks, <math id="S2.SS11.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Frust</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math> was significantly higher in CBM1 than in CBM2 (paired Wilcoxon tests <math id="S2.SS11.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.0488" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0488</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.0488</annotation></semantics></math>, Figures <a href="#S2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 2.11 Resolving concept frustration in real-world language and vision tasks ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>A&amp;B). In contrast, the Euclidean analogue <math id="S2.SS11.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Frust}^{E}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Frust</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Frust}^{E}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math> did not differ between models. Further results are provided in Supplementary Tables S3&amp;4.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS11.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS11.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These results indicate that when a task-relevant frustrating concept is omitted, the model learns representations of the remaining concepts that are geometrically inconsistent with the fixed unsupervised feature basis. When the frustrating concept is incorporated into the supervised ontology, the learned representation of the shared concepts reorganises and becomes less frustrated with the same SAE directions in the task-aligned geometry. Thus, incorporating a previously hidden concept does not merely improve ontology completeness; it changes how the model internally represents relationships between existing concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS11.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS11.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This mirrors how human conceptual understanding can change when a previously unrecognised explanatory factor is introduced, reorganising relationships between existing concepts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29654v1/fig_5_real_wordl_smaller.png" id="S2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/434;" width="476" height="434" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="S2.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The value of frustration in real world language and vision tasks.</span>
Box and violin plots display <math id="S2.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>Frust</mtext><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Frust}^{E}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>Frust</mtext><mi>E</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Frust}^{E}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math> for CBM1 and CBM2, calculated from 10 fold cross-validation of models trained to predict (A) a language task: sarcasm detection and (B) a vision task: discriminating pictures of gulls from terns. Paired Wilcoxon <math id="S2.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values and 95% confidence intervals of the median difference between metric values between CBM1 and CBM2 are displayed. We see that <math id="S2.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}_{CBM1}(1,2)&gt;\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}_{CBM2}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Frust</mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Frust</mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}_{CBM1}(1,2)&gt;\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}_{CBM2}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math> in both settings while <math id="S2.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Frust}^{E}_{CBM1}(1,2)=\textrm{Frust}^{E}_{CBM2}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Frust</mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext>Frust</mtext><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>E</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Frust}^{E}_{CBM1}(1,2)=\textrm{Frust}^{E}_{CBM2}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alignment between human-interpretable reasoning and machine representations remains a central challenge in explainable AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. A substantial body of work assumes that unsupervised machine representations are only valuable when orthogonal to human-defined concepts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. Our results suggest a different perspective: machine-derived concepts can instead induce relationships between known concepts that contradict their expected semantics, motivating a reorganisation of the geometry of the known concept space. We term such concepts <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">frustrating</em>. Their presence degrades both accuracy and interpretability of supervised concept models. In real-world vision and language tasks, incorporating a frustrating concept into a supervised ontology reorganises learned representations of known concepts, restoring geometric consistency between human and machine representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This process resembles how conceptual change occurs in scientific discovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, where progress often arises not by introducing independent variables but by discovering a concept that forces reinterpretation of existing knowledge. Recognising that the Earth is round rather than flat resolves
contradictions in geodesic distances observed when moving in depth. Similarly, Einstein’s theory of relativity reorganised the classical understanding of space, time, and motion, resolving tensions that could not be explained within Newtonian mechanics. Our results suggest interpretable machine learning systems may undergo an analogous process: the discovery or inclusion of a previously hidden concept can reorganise the internal geometry of supervised concept representations and resolve contradictions between human and machine reasoning. We formalise this via the notion of concept frustration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Though encouraging, our results require some considerations. First, our theoretical analysis relies on a linear–Gaussian generative model which enables analytical tractability. This is not an unrealistic assumption: CBMs typically employ a linear mapping from concepts to outcomes to preserve interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, and foundation models are often assumed to provide embeddings from which relevant concepts are approximately linearly recoverable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. However, real-world representation spaces may exhibit more complex associations. Second, our measure of pairwise frustration depends on selecting a maximally frustrating unsupervised direction from the SAE dictionary. While this choice captures the strongest geometric contradiction between concept directions, alternative strategies may provide complementary insights. Third, the task-aligned geometry is derived from a quadratic form based on the Fisher information metric of a simple black-box classifier. While this construction provides an analytically tractable geometry, more complex predictive models may induce richer structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Promising directions of future work include extending the theory beyond linear–Gaussian settings, analysing higher-order structures in concept similarity matrices, elaborating the connection to frustration in spin systems, and applying frustration-based diagnostics to large foundation models. Our perspective also motivates new model architectures that combine supervised concepts with unsupervised concept discovery. More broadly, viewing interpretability through the lens of geometric consistency between human concepts and machine representations provides a principled framework for identifying missing conceptual dimensions and refining ontologies through which both humans and machines reason about complex systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methods</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Model architectures</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Black box model.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We employ a feedforward architecture with a single hidden layer of width <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and ReLU activation,</p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(\mathbf{a})=\mathrm{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{H}\mathbf{a}+b_{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ReLU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(\mathbf{a})=\mathrm{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{H}\mathbf{a}+b_{H})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle l(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{w}_{l}^{\top}H(\mathbf{a})+b_{l}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle l(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{w}_{l}^{\top}H(\mathbf{a})+b_{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{y}=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{y}=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:\mathbb{R}^{r}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:\mathbb{R}^{r}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l:\mathbb{R}^{h}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l:\mathbb{R}^{h}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned matrix of weights and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned vector of weights. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned vector of biases and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{l}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{l}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned bias.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For binary tasks, parameters are learned by minimizing the empirical binary cross-entropy loss</p>
<table id="S4.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{BB}}=\mathbb{E}\big[\ell_{\text{BCE}}(\hat{y}(\mathbf{a}),y)\big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>BB</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mtext>BCE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{BB}}=\mathbb{E}\big[\ell_{\text{BCE}}(\hat{y}(\mathbf{a}),y)\big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sparse Autoencoder.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SAEs are trained by minimizing</p>
<table id="S4.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SAE}}=\mathbb{E}\big[\|\mathbf{a}-\hat{\mathbf{a}}\|_{2}^{2}\big]+\lambda_{\text{SAE}}\,\mathbb{E}\big[\|s(\mathbf{a})\|_{1}\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SAE}}=\mathbb{E}\big[\|\mathbf{a}-\hat{\mathbf{a}}\|_{2}^{2}\big]+\lambda_{\text{SAE}}\,\mathbb{E}\big[\|s(\mathbf{a})\|_{1}\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{SAE}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>SAE</mtext></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{SAE}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> controls sparsity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Concept Bottleneck Model.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We parameterise CBMs with a linear concept mapping</p>
<table id="S4.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\text{known}}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{a}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝐐𝐚</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\text{known}}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{a},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{known}}\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐐</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\text{known}}\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>. The predicted concepts are then passed to a linear task head <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, and the final binary prediction <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained via a sigmoid transformation:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(\mathbf{a})=\sigma(\mathbf{w}_{\textrm{CBM}}^{\top}\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\text{known}}(\mathbf{a})+b_{\textrm{CBM}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>CBM</mtext><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mtext>CBM</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\mathbf{a})=\sigma(\mathbf{w}_{\textrm{CBM}}^{\top}\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\text{known}}(\mathbf{a})+b_{\textrm{CBM}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider CBM joint training, where concept and task losses are optimized simultaneously:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{joint}}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}(y,\hat{y})+\lambda_{C}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{concept}}(\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{known}},\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\mathrm{known}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>joint</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>concept</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>known</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{joint}}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}(y,\hat{y})+\lambda_{C}\,\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{concept}}(\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{known}},\hat{\mathbf{c}}_{\mathrm{known}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S4.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{concept}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left\|\mathbf{c}^{(i)}_{\mathrm{known}}-\hat{\mathbf{c}}^{(i)}_{\mathrm{known}}\right\|_{2}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>concept</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>known</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>known</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{concept}}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left\|\mathbf{c}^{(i)}_{\mathrm{known}}-\hat{\mathbf{c}}^{(i)}_{\mathrm{known}}\right\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>task</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{task}}</annotation></semantics></math> is binary cross-entropy, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{C}</annotation></semantics></math> balances concept prediction and task prediction.
Both the concept predictor and task head are updated jointly.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Proof of Theorem 1</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we prove the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheoremx1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheoremx1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmtheoremx1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Closed-form Fisher metric for one-hidden-layer binary model)<span id="Thmtheoremx1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheoremx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheoremx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the black-box model defined above with sigmoid output. Then the Fisher information metric on activation space admits the closed-form expression</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=p(\mathbf{a})(1-p(\mathbf{a}))\,g(\mathbf{a})g(\mathbf{a})^{\top}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=p(\mathbf{a})(1-p(\mathbf{a}))\,g(\mathbf{a})g(\mathbf{a})^{\top},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheoremx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheoremx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{a})\equiv p(y=1|\mathbf{a})=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{a})\equiv p(y=1|\mathbf{a})=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{a})=\nabla_{a}l(\mathbf{a})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>a</mi></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{a})=\nabla_{a}l(\mathbf{a}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Consider how the black box model is trained, we have an input <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}\in\mathbb{R}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> which is transformed by a hidden layer according to a function <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H:\mathbb{R}^{r}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H:\mathbb{R}^{r}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathbf{a})=\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{H}\mathbf{a}+b_{H})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathbf{a})=\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{H}\mathbf{a}+b_{H}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{h\times r}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned matrix of weights and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{H}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned vector of biases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">This layer is then transformed to a logit via</span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{w}_{l}^{\top}H(\mathbf{a})+b_{l}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{w}_{l}^{\top}H(\mathbf{a})+b_{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>h</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned vector of weights and <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{l}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{l}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned bias.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">This is then converted into a probability via a sigmoid:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(y=1|\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(y=1|\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(y=0|\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(y=0|\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We want to calculate:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{a})}\bigg[\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})^{\top}\bigg]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim p(y|\mathbf{a})}\bigg[\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})^{\top}\bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since the model defines a Bernoulli distribution over <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log p(y|\mathbf{a})=y\log\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))+(1-y)\log(1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log p(y|\mathbf{a})=y\log\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))+(1-y)\log(1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">hence, using the identity for sigmoid functions <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(l(\mathbf{a}))=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))(1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(l(\mathbf{a}))=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))(1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})=(y-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}\log p(y|\mathbf{a})=(y-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">Taking expectations over <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F_{A}(\mathbf{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F_{A}(\mathbf{a})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[((y-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))^{2}]\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mi>l</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[((y-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))^{2}]\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))(1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))(1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.5.1" class="ltx_text">which follows as <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[((y-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))^{2}]=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))(1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[((y-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))^{2}]=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))(1-\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))</annotation></semantics></math> is the variance of the Bernoulli distribution <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Ber}(\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Ber</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Ber}(\sigma(l(\mathbf{a})))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall</span></p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{w}_{l}^{\top}\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{H}\mathbf{a}+b_{H})+b_{l}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>H</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{w}_{l}^{\top}\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_{H}\mathbf{a}+b_{H})+b_{l},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">noting that <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\frac{\partial\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{z})}{\partial z_{j}}=\mathds{1}\{z_{j}&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mtext>ReLU</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial\text{ReLU}(\mathbf{z})}{\partial z_{j}}=\mathds{1}\{z_{j}&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and defining</span></p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="m_{i}(\mathbf{a})=\mathds{1}\{(\mathbf{W}_{H}\mathbf{a}+b_{H})_{i}&gt;0\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}(\mathbf{a})=\mathds{1}\{(\mathbf{W}_{H}\mathbf{a}+b_{H})_{i}&gt;0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">we deduce</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial l(\mathbf{a})}{\partial a_{i}}=\sum_{j=1}^{h}(\mathbf{w}_{l})_{j}m_{j}(\mathbf{a})(\mathbf{W}_{H})_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>h</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial l(\mathbf{a})}{\partial a_{i}}=\sum_{j=1}^{h}(\mathbf{w}_{l})_{j}m_{j}(\mathbf{a})(\mathbf{W}_{H})_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.4.1" class="ltx_text">or</span></p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{W}_{H}^{\top}(m(\mathbf{a})\odot\mathbf{w}_{l})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐚</mi></msub><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{a}}l(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{W}_{H}^{\top}(m(\mathbf{a})\odot\mathbf{w}_{l}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p6.5.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{a}\odot\mathbf{b})_{j}=a_{j}b_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{a}\odot\mathbf{b})_{j}=a_{j}b_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Hadamard product.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=p(\mathbf{a})(1-p(\mathbf{a}))g(\mathbf{a})g(\mathbf{a})^{\top}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{A}(\mathbf{a})=p(\mathbf{a})(1-p(\mathbf{a}))g(\mathbf{a})g(\mathbf{a})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{a})=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{a})=\sigma(l(\mathbf{a}))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{W}_{H}^{\top}(m(\mathbf{a})\odot\mathbf{w}_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>H</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\mathbf{a})=\mathbf{W}_{H}^{\top}(m(\mathbf{a})\odot\mathbf{w}_{l})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Globe treasure hunter data generation and training protocol</h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Geometric settings.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider two geometric settings: a cylindrical (“flatworld”) geometry and a spherical (“round-world”) geometry. In both settings, points <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> represent possible treasure locations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spherical geometry.</span>
Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}^{2}=\{\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}:\|\mathbf{u}\|_{2}=1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}^{2}=\{\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}:\|\mathbf{u}\|_{2}=1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the unit sphere. To generate a point:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample a surface direction <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\mathbb{S}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\mathbb{S}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample a radial depth <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\sim\mathrm{Unif}([0,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\sim\mathrm{Unif}([0,1))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define the final location as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex41" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}=(1-d)\mathbf{u}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}=(1-d)\mathbf{u}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=0</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to the surface of the unit sphere, and increasing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> moves radially inward to a sphere of radius <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cylindrical geometry.</span>
Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}^{2}=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}:x^{2}+y^{2}\leq 1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}^{2}=\{(x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^{2}:x^{2}+y^{2}\leq 1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the unit disk. To generate a point:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample a surface location <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{u},y_{u})\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\mathbb{D}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{u},y_{u})\sim\mathrm{Unif}(\mathbb{D}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sample a vertical depth <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\sim\mathrm{Unif}([0,1))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\sim\mathrm{Unif}([0,1))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define</p>
<table id="S4.Ex42" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}=(x_{u},y_{u},-d)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}=(x_{u},y_{u},-d).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In this setting, the Earth’s surface corresponds to the plane <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=0</annotation></semantics></math>, and increasing <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> translates orthogonally away from the plane without altering its intrinsic disk geometry. The North and South poles correspond to the loci <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=-1</annotation></semantics></math> respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Concept definitions.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In both geometries we define three concepts. Let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=1-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=1-d</annotation></semantics></math> denote the radius of the spherical cross-section at depth <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervised concepts <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We define <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as surface-parallel distances to the North and South poles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the spherical geometry, these are geodesic distances on the sphere of radius <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=1-d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=1-d</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{1}(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{1}(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\rho\cdot\arccos\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{p}\cdot(0,\rho,0)}{\rho^{2}}\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.657em;" width="2.657em"><mi>arccos</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\rho\cdot\arccos\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{p}\cdot(0,\rho,0)}{\rho^{2}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{2}(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{2}(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\rho\cdot\arccos\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{p}\cdot(0,-\rho,0)}{\rho^{2}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:2.657em;" width="2.657em"><mi>arccos</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\rho\cdot\arccos\!\left(\frac{\mathbf{p}\cdot(0,-\rho,0)}{\rho^{2}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}=\rho\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐮</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}=\rho\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{S}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐮</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}\in\mathbb{S}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, these simplify to</p>
<table id="S4.Ex43" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}(\mathbf{p})=\rho\arccos(u_{y}),\qquad C_{2}(\mathbf{p})=\rho\arccos(-u_{y})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arccos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}(\mathbf{p})=\rho\arccos(u_{y}),\qquad C_{2}(\mathbf{p})=\rho\arccos(-u_{y}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the cylindrical geometry, these are intrinsic (planar) distances within the disk at fixed depth:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{1}(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{1}(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{x^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{x^{2}+(y-1)^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{2}(\mathbf{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{2}(\mathbf{p})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sqrt{x^{2}+(y+1)^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sqrt{x^{2}+(y+1)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unsupervised concept.</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C_{3}(\mathbf{p})=d\quad\text{(depth)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>(depth)</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C_{3}(\mathbf{p})=d\quad\text{(depth)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Only <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> are treated as supervised concepts. Depth <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is unobserved by the CBM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Concept-to-activation mapping.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each location <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐩</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define the concept vector</p>
<table id="S4.Ex44" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}^{(\mathbf{p})}=(C_{1}(\mathbf{p}),C_{2}(\mathbf{p}),C_{3}(\mathbf{p}))^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}^{(\mathbf{p})}=(C_{1}(\mathbf{p}),C_{2}(\mathbf{p}),C_{3}(\mathbf{p}))^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Activations are generated via a linear–Gaussian model:</p>
<table id="S4.E32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}^{(\mathbf{p})}=\boldsymbol{\Phi}\mathbf{c}^{(\mathbf{p})}+\boldsymbol{\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}^{(\mathbf{p})}=\boldsymbol{\Phi}\mathbf{c}^{(\mathbf{p})}+\boldsymbol{\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where:</p>
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times 3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times 3}</annotation></semantics></math> has entries independently sampled from <math id="S4.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{a}^{2}\mathbf{I}_{r})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{a}^{2}\mathbf{I}_{r})</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=64</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the activation dimension.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thus the activation space is a noisy linear embedding of the three underlying concepts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Treasure hunter task.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We fix a reference point on the equator,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex45" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}=(1,0,0)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}=(1,0,0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and define the binary classification task</p>
<table id="S4.E33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y(\mathbf{p})=\mathds{1}\{\|\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{e}\|_{2}&lt;R\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐩</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y(\mathbf{p})=\mathds{1}\{\|\mathbf{p}-\mathbf{e}\|_{2}&lt;R\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=0.75</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset construction.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each geometry, we generate <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8000</annotation></semantics></math> samples. Data are split into training and test sets in a 75:25 ratio. All reported results are averaged over 50 independent repetitions, each with independently sampled <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and random train/test splits.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model training.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S4.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A one-hidden-layer black-box model with width <math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=128</annotation></semantics></math> using binary cross-entropy loss.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A sparse autoencoder with <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{SAE}}=60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{SAE}}=60</annotation></semantics></math> latent units and sparsity coefficient <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{SAE}}=10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{SAE}}=10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A jointly trained CBM with concept loss weight <math id="S4.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{C}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{C}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All models are trained using the Adam optimiser <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> with batch size <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math>.
The black-box model and CBM are trained with learning rate <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> epochs. The SAE is trained with learning rate <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> for
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="60" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>60</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">60</annotation></semantics></math> epochs, as unsupervised feature discovery typically requires longer training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fisher geometry.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Fisher metric is computed using the closed-form expression derived in Theorem 1. To obtain a stable task-aligned quadratic form, we average over activations satisfying</p>
<table id="S4.Ex46" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\mathbf{a})\in(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\mathbf{a})\in(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.2" class="ltx_p">yielding <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{F}_{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>A</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{F}_{A}</annotation></semantics></math> as defined in Equation (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2.3 A task-aligned geometry to compare supervised and unsupervised concepts ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We examined several intervals for <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})\in\{(0,1),(0.1,0.9),(0.2,0.8),(0.3,0.7),(0.4,0.6)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})\in\{(0,1),(0.1,0.9),(0.2,0.8),(0.3,0.7),(0.4,0.6)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and investigated their impact on the difference between <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the cylindrical vs the spherical geometries (Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Fisher geometry. ‣ 4.3 Globe treasure hunter data generation and training protocol ‣ 4 Methods ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). We found that the results were qualitatively comparable across different choices of interval, but quantitatively strongest <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})=(0.2,0.8)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})=(0.2,0.8)</annotation></semantics></math>, so we selected this interval for all experiments in the manuscript for consistency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.29654v1/fisher_window_sensitivity_two_panel_SI.png" id="S4.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/198;" width="476" height="198" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="S4.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of decision-boundary window size on frustration detection.</span> Left: Bar plot shows <math id="S4.F7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log_{10}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log_{10}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> for the corresponding paired Wilcoxon tests comparing <math id="S4.F7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> between spherical and cylindrical geometries. Right: Violin and box plots show the paired differences <math id="S4.F7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\gamma^{F_{A}}=\gamma^{F_{A}}_{\mathrm{sphere}}-\gamma^{F_{A}}_{\mathrm{cylinder}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>sphere</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>cylinder</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\gamma^{F_{A}}=\gamma^{F_{A}}_{\mathrm{sphere}}-\gamma^{F_{A}}_{\mathrm{cylinder}}</annotation></semantics></math> across 50 simulations for different decision-boundary windows <math id="S4.F7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})</annotation></semantics></math> used to compute the Fisher metric. Across all boundary windows, <math id="S4.F7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma^{F_{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>γ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma^{F_{A}}</annotation></semantics></math> is consistently higher in the spherical geometry than in the cylindrical geometry, indicating robust detection of concept frustration. The effect is quantitatively strongest for the intermediate window <math id="S4.F7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})=(0.2,0.8)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>low</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>high</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p_{\mathrm{low}},p_{\mathrm{high}})=(0.2,0.8)</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Statistical testing.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each metric, comparisons between cylindrical and spherical geometries are performed using a Wilcoxon signed-rank test across 50 repetitions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Synthetic data generation and and construction of the structured interaction matrix <math id="S4.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S2.SS6" title="2.6 A data model to control concept frustration ‣ 2 Results ‣ Concept frustration: Aligning human concepts and machine representations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>, we generated synthetic data from a linear-Gaussian concept model with controllable interactions between supervised and unsupervised concepts. We denote the full concept vector by</p>
<table id="S4.Ex47" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}=(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u})\in\mathbb{R}^{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝝌</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}=(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u})\in\mathbb{R}^{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{known}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{known}}}</annotation></semantics></math> contains the supervised (known) concepts and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{unknown}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{unknown}}}</annotation></semantics></math> the unsupervised (unknown) concepts, with <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=k_{\mathrm{known}}+k_{\mathrm{unknown}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=k_{\mathrm{known}}+k_{\mathrm{unknown}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first sampled a fixed symmetric positive definite covariance matrix</p>
<table id="S4.Ex48" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{known}}\times k_{\mathrm{known}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{known}}\times k_{\mathrm{known}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which defines the covariance structure among known concepts, and a fixed symmetric positive definite base covariance</p>
<table id="S4.Ex49" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex49.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{unknown}}\times k_{\mathrm{unknown}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>temp</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{unknown}}\times k_{\mathrm{unknown}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">which captures the component of unknown-concept variation intrinsic to the unknown concepts. In the notation of the Results, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{kk}\equiv\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{kk}\equiv\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To control semantic consistency versus frustration between known and unknown concepts, we constructed an <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent interaction matrix</p>
<table id="S4.Ex50" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\alpha)\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{known}}\times k_{\mathrm{unknown}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\alpha)\in\mathbb{R}^{k_{\mathrm{known}}\times k_{\mathrm{unknown}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the sign of <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> selects whether unknown concepts reinforce (<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) or oppose (<math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>) the correlation structure among known concepts, and <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\alpha|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\alpha|</annotation></semantics></math> controls interaction strength.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Let</p>
<table id="S4.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{ij}=(\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}})_{ij},\qquad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq k_{\mathrm{known}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{ij}=(\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}})_{ij},\qquad 1\leq i&lt;j\leq k_{\mathrm{known}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Each unknown concept index <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u\in\{1,\dots,k_{\mathrm{unknown}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\in\{1,\dots,k_{\mathrm{unknown}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> was assigned to mediate one selected known pair <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i(u),j(u))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i(u),j(u))</annotation></semantics></math>. This assignment rule was fixed across all values of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, and all remaining entries of the corresponding column were set to zero. For each such <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>, we set two nonzero entries in column <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="M(\alpha)_{i(u),\,u}=b_{i(u)j(u)}|\alpha|,\qquad M(\alpha)_{j(u),\,u}=-\,\mathrm{sign}(\alpha)\cdot|b_{i(u)j(u)}|\cdot|\alpha|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>α</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>M</mi><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.392em">−</mo><mi>sign</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>α</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(\alpha)_{i(u),\,u}=b_{i(u)j(u)}|\alpha|,\qquad M(\alpha)_{j(u),\,u}=-\,\mathrm{sign}(\alpha)\cdot|b_{i(u)j(u)}|\cdot|\alpha|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">At <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>, this construction gives <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(0)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(0)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so known and unknown concepts are independent. In the notation of the Results, we identify</p>
<table id="S4.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\equiv\mathbf{M}(\alpha),\qquad\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\equiv\mathbf{M}(\alpha)^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\equiv\mathbf{M}(\alpha),\qquad\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\equiv\mathbf{M}(\alpha)^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>, we defined the unknown-concept covariance as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)=\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}+\mathbf{M}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}^{-1}\mathbf{M}(\alpha)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>temp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)=\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}+\mathbf{M}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}^{-1}\mathbf{M}(\alpha),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">and assembled the full concept covariance matrix as</p>
<table id="S4.E34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}(\alpha)=\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}&amp;\mathbf{M}(\alpha)\\
\mathbf{M}(\alpha)^{\top}&amp;\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}+\mathbf{M}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}^{-1}\mathbf{M}(\alpha)\end{pmatrix}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>temp</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐌</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}(\alpha)=\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}&amp;\mathbf{M}(\alpha)\\
\mathbf{M}(\alpha)^{\top}&amp;\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{temp}}+\mathbf{M}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}^{-1}\mathbf{M}(\alpha)\end{pmatrix}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.3" class="ltx_p">By Schur complement, <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is symmetric positive definite for all <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Cov}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})=\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Cov</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Cov}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})=\mathbf{B}_{\mathrm{known}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p7.4" class="ltx_p">is preserved across regimes, so varying <math id="S4.SS4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> changes only the coupling between known and unknown concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We then generated <math id="S4.SS4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> independent concept samples according to</p>
<table id="S4.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B}(\alpha)),\qquad i=1,\dots,n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝝌</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B}(\alpha)),\qquad i=1,\dots,n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Ground-truth task labels were generated from concepts through a noisy linear map,</p>
<table id="S4.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}^{(i)}=\boldsymbol{\Phi}\boldsymbol{\chi}^{(i)}+\boldsymbol{\epsilon}^{(i)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝐚</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝚽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝝌</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}^{(i)}=\boldsymbol{\Phi}\boldsymbol{\chi}^{(i)}+\boldsymbol{\epsilon}^{(i)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝚽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Phi}\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}</annotation></semantics></math> has entries independently sampled from <math id="S4.SS4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S4.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{a}^{2}\,\mathbf{I}_{r})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{a}^{2}\,\mathbf{I}_{r}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p9.3" class="ltx_p">The parameter <math id="S4.SS4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{a}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{a}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> therefore controls the difficulty of recovering concepts from activations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">To construct the task, we first sampled a base task vector</p>
<table id="S4.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\ast}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{k})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝝍</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\ast}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{I}_{k}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p10.2" class="ltx_p">and defined</p>
<table id="S4.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}=\big((1-\omega)\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\ast}_{1:k_{\mathrm{known}}},\;\omega\,\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\ast}_{k_{\mathrm{known}}+1:k}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝍</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>ω</mi><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}=\big((1-\omega)\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\ast}_{1:k_{\mathrm{known}}},\;\omega\,\boldsymbol{\psi}^{\ast}_{k_{\mathrm{known}}+1:k}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p10.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S4.SS4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Writing <math id="S4.SS4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}=(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝍</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}=(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})</annotation></semantics></math>, the parameter <math id="S4.SS4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> controls the extent to which the task depends on known versus unknown concepts: <math id="S4.SS4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=0</annotation></semantics></math> yields a task depending only on known concepts, whereas <math id="S4.SS4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega=1</annotation></semantics></math> yields a task depending only on unknown concepts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we defined the latent task score</p>
<table id="S4.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau^{(i)}=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}^{(i)}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}^{(i)}+\eta^{(i)},\qquad\eta^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau^{(i)}=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}^{(i)}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}^{(i)}+\eta^{(i)},\qquad\eta^{(i)}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p11.2" class="ltx_p">and generated binary labels as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y^{(i)}=\mathds{1}\{\tau^{(i)}&gt;0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{(i)}=\mathds{1}\{\tau^{(i)}&gt;0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p11.3" class="ltx_p">The parameter <math id="S4.SS4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the intrinsic noise of the task and therefore the difficulty of predicting <math id="S4.SS4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> even from the full concept set.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Proof of Theorem 2</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we prove the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheoremx2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem</span></span><span id="Thmtheoremx2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Concept-optimal Accuracy in the Linear–Gaussian Model)<span id="Thmtheoremx2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheoremx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the concept vector be denoted <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}=(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝛘</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝛘</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}=(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}=(c_{1},\cdots,c_{k_{\mathrm{known}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛘</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}=(c_{1},\cdots,c_{k_{\mathrm{known}}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes known concepts and <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}=(c_{k_{\mathrm{known}}+1},\cdots,c_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛘</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}=(c_{k_{\mathrm{known}}+1},\cdots,c_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> unknown concepts, under our model <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}=(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u})\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝛘</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝛘</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}=(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u})\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B})</annotation></semantics></math> with block covariance</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{B}_{kk}&amp;\mathbf{B}_{ku}\\
\mathbf{B}_{uk}&amp;\mathbf{B}_{uu}\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}=\begin{pmatrix}\mathbf{B}_{kk}&amp;\mathbf{B}_{ku}\\
\mathbf{B}_{uk}&amp;\mathbf{B}_{uu}\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheoremx2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)=\mathbf{B}_{temp}+\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{uu}(\alpha)=\mathbf{B}_{temp}+\mathbf{B}_{uk}(\alpha)\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{kk}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{kk}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive definite (and thus invertible).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheoremx2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the weight vector be denoted <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}=(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝛙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝛙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝛙</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}=(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}=(w_{1},\cdots,w_{k_{\mathrm{known}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛙</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}=(w_{1},\cdots,w_{k_{\mathrm{known}}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes known concept-task weights and <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}=(w_{k_{\mathrm{known}}+1},\cdots,w_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛙</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}=(w_{k_{\mathrm{known}}+1},\cdots,w_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> unknown concept-task weights.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheoremx2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the task variable be</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}+\eta,\qquad\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}+\eta,\qquad\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and define the binary label</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y=\mathds{1}\{\tau&gt;0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\mathds{1}\{\tau&gt;0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheoremx2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then the concept-optimal Bayes classifier using only the known concepts <math id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛘</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which minimises the risk</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R(\hat{y})=\mathbb{E}_{(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},y)}\left[\mathds{1}\{\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})\neq y\}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\hat{y})=\mathbb{E}_{(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},y)}\left[\mathds{1}\{\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})\neq y\}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})=\mathds{1}\{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}&gt;0\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})=\mathds{1}\{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}&gt;0\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and its classification accuracy is</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha)=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\left(\sqrt{\frac{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)}{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}}}\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Acc</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">CBM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></msqrt><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha)=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\left(\sqrt{\frac{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)}{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}}}\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheoremx2.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\mathbf{B}_{ku}(\alpha)\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">To compute the optimal classifier given known concepts, we must calculate the conditional distribution <math id="S4.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We note that as our toy model is linear-Gaussian this conditional distribution will also be Gaussian and can thus be explicitly calculated via <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Var}(\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Var</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Var}(\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first note that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex71"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex71.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}+\eta|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}+\eta|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex72"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex72.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex73"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex73.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex74"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex74.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex75"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex75.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\phi}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Which follows from linearity of expectation, the fact that <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}_{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, the fact that <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝌</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B})</annotation></semantics></math> and the conditional distribution of multivariate Gaussian distributions, and where we have defined <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}:=\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}:=\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> is a function of our frustration parameter <math id="S4.SS5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now consider</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex76"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex76.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Var}[\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Var}[\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex76.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Var}[\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}+\eta|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Var}[\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}+\eta|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex77"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex77.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\text{Var}[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\text{Var}[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex78"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex78.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex79"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex79.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{\text{temp}}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mtext>temp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{\text{temp}}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Which follows from the variance of a constant being zero the fact that <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}_{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}_{y})</annotation></semantics></math> and is an independent variable, the fact that <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝌</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐁</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B})</annotation></semantics></math>, the conditional distribution of multivariate Gaussian distributions, and the construction of <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}</annotation></semantics></math> which ensures <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{B}_{\text{temp}}=\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mtext>temp</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{B}_{\text{temp}}=\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku}</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that via our construction this variance is not dependent on <math id="S4.SS5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>, the frustration parameter.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Whence <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}\sim\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{\text{temp}}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mtext>temp</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}\sim\mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{\text{temp}}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
By definition the concept-optimal Bayes predictor is the risk minimiser</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\hat{y})=\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}},y)}\left[\ell(\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}),y)\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\hat{y})=\mathbb{E}_{(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}},y)}\left[\ell(\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}),y)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">with respect to the 0-1 loss.
Since <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}),y)\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}),y)\mid\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}\right]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}),y)\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}),y)\mid\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}\right]\right]</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is minimised by setting</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}})=\mathrm{argmin}_{z\in\{0,1\}}\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(z,y)\mid\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}})=\mathrm{argmin}_{z\in\{0,1\}}\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(z,y)\mid\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">When <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l(z,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l(z,y)</annotation></semantics></math> is the 0-1 loss,
we obtain as usual
<math id="S4.SS5.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}})=\mathrm{argmax}_{z}\mathbb{P}(Y=z|\chi_{k}=\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>argmax</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}})=\mathrm{argmax}_{z}\mathbb{P}(Y=z|\chi_{k}=\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence a concept-optimal model will predict <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math>
iff
<math id="S4.SS5.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(Y=1|\chi_{k}=\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}})&gt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(Y=1|\chi_{k}=\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}})&gt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math>,
and thus
will predict <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\tau&gt;0|\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}]&gt;1/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mtext>known</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\tau&gt;0|\mathbf{c}_{\text{known}}]&gt;1/2</annotation></semantics></math> and zero otherwise.
It therefore follows that the optimal classifier given <math id="S4.SS5.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is simply:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})=\mathds{1}\{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}&gt;0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})=\mathds{1}\{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}&gt;0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">As the true outcome is <math id="S4.SS5.p7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y=\mathds{1}\{\tau&gt;0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=\mathds{1}\{\tau&gt;0\}</annotation></semantics></math> We note that the optimal classifier makes an error when <math id="S4.SS5.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{sign}(\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})\not=\textrm{sign}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{sign}(\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})\not=\textrm{sign}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us define <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:=\mathbb{E}[\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]=\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:=\mathbb{E}[\tau|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]=\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:=\tau-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:=\tau-S</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that as <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B}_{kk})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B}_{kk})</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha))</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{S}^{2}=\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{S}^{2}=\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>. We also note that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex82"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex82.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\tau-S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\tau-S</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex83"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex83.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\chi_{u}+\eta-(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>χ</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}+\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\chi_{u}+\eta-(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}+\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u})^{\top}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex84"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})+\eta." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})+\eta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">It is clear that <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[R]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[R]=0</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex85"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex85.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Var}[R]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Var}[R]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex85.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}))^{2}]+2\mathbb{E}[\eta]\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})]]+\mathbb{E}[\eta^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}))^{2}]+2\mathbb{E}[\eta]\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})]]+\mathbb{E}[\eta^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex86"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex86.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}))^{2}]+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}))^{2}]+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex87"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex87.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})^{2}]\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}_{kk}^{-1}\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k})^{2}]\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex88"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex88.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}])^{2}]\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbb{E}[(\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}-\mathbb{E}[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}|\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}])^{2}]\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex89"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex89.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}\mid\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Var</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathrm{Var}[\boldsymbol{\chi}_{u}\mid\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}]\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex90"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex90.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}B^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}B^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS5.p8.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p8.3.1" class="ltx_text">which follows from the properties of a multivariate Gaussian. Thus <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote <math id="S4.SS5.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{R}^{2}=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{R}^{2}=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Thus the accuracy of the Bayes optimal classifier is <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}[\textrm{sign}(S)=\textrm{sign}(S+R)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>sign</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>sign</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}[\textrm{sign}(S)=\textrm{sign}(S+R)]</annotation></semantics></math>. We note that <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> are independent Gaussian variables (i.e., <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Cov}(R,S)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Cov</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Cov}(R,S)=0</annotation></semantics></math>) and that <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Var}(S+R)=\sigma_{R}^{2}+\sigma_{S}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Var}(S+R)=\sigma_{R}^{2}+\sigma_{S}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Cov}(S,S+R)=\sigma_{S}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Cov</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Cov}(S,S+R)=\sigma_{S}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus the correlation co-efficient between <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S+R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S+R</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, is <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{S,S+R}=\frac{\sigma_{S}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{S}^{2}+\sigma_{R}^{2}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{S,S+R}=\frac{\sigma_{S}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{S}^{2}+\sigma_{R}^{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It can therefore be demonstrated through simple integration that the accuracy of the Bayes-optimal CBM given <math id="S4.SS5.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝌</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\chi}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex91"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex91.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[\textrm{sign}(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mtext>sign</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}[\textrm{sign}(S)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex91.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\textrm{sign}(S+R)]=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arcsin\rho_{S,S+R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>sign</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mi>arcsin</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\textrm{sign}(S+R)]=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arcsin\rho_{S,S+R}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex92"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex92.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arcsin\Bigg(\frac{\sigma_{S}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{S}^{2}+\sigma_{R}^{2}}}\Bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arcsin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arcsin\Bigg(\frac{\sigma_{S}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{S}^{2}+\sigma_{R}^{2}}}\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex93"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex93.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\Bigg(\frac{\sigma_{S}}{\sigma_{R}}\Bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\Bigg(\frac{\sigma_{S}}{\sigma_{R}}\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex94"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex94.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\Bigg(\sqrt{\frac{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)}{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}}}\Bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\Bigg(\sqrt{\frac{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)}{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}(\mathbf{B}_{uu}-\mathbf{B}_{uk}\mathbf{B}^{-1}_{kk}\mathbf{B}_{ku})\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}}}\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex95"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex95.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\Bigg(\sqrt{\frac{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)}{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}}}\Bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐁</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{\pi}\arctan\Bigg(\sqrt{\frac{\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{kk}\boldsymbol{\phi}(\alpha)}{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{B}_{temp}\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}+\sigma_{y}^{2}}}\Bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS5.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We denote this quantity as <math id="S4.SS5.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Acc</mtext><mtext>CBM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Acc}_{\textrm{CBM}}(\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Experiments with synthetic data</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used the linear-Gaussian synthetic data generator defined above to study interactions between supervised (“known”) and unsupervised (“unknown”) concepts under controlled covariance structure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each simulation we generated:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex96"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex96.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex96.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=8000," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>8000</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=8000,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex97"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex97.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex97.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=50," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=50,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex98"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex98.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{\mathrm{known}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{\mathrm{known}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex98.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\in\{10,20,30,40\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\in\{10,20,30,40\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex99"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex99.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=64," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=64,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex100"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{a}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex100.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0.3," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0.3,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex101"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sigma_{y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sigma_{y}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex101.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=1.5." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=1.5.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Concepts were sampled as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex102" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex102.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}=(\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{known}},\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{unknown}})\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B}(\alpha))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>known</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝐁</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}=(\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{known}},\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{unknown}})\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\mathbf{B}(\alpha)),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS6.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{-1,0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{-1,0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> controls semantic alignment between known and unknown concepts:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S4.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=-1</annotation></semantics></math>: semantic alignment (consistent)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0</annotation></semantics></math>: independence</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=+1</annotation></semantics></math>: semantic opposition (frustration)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The outcome was generated as</p>
<table id="S4.Ex103" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex103.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{known}}+\omega\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{unknown}}+\varepsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>known</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>unknown</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{k}^{\top}\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{known}}+\omega\boldsymbol{\psi}_{u}^{\top}\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{unknown}}+\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS6.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega\in\{0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.75</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega\in\{0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the proportion of task signal arising from unknown concepts and <math id="S4.SS6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{y}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Observed features were generated via a linear projection</p>
<table id="S4.Ex104" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex104.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{c}+\eta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝐀𝐜</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\mathbf{A}\mathbf{c}+\eta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS6.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{a}^{2}I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{a}^{2}I)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Each of the 60 parameter regimes was simulated across 10 independent seeds (600 datasets total). Data were split 75:25 into train and test sets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.7 </span>Real-world experiments</h3>

<section id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Language experiment (sarcasm detection).</h4>

<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dataset and embeddings.</span>
We used the News Headlines sarcasm dataset (28,000 headlines; 13,000 labelled sarcastic) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Each headline was embedded using DeBERTa-v3-base, producing a 768-dimensional representation <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Concept construction.</span>
We defined three continuous sentiment concepts derived from RoBERTa-base-sentiment logits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>: <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (positive sentiment), <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (negative sentiment), and <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (sentiment intensity), defined as the negative neutrality logit. In this dataset, <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are inversely correlated, while <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is positively correlated with both. A linear model confirmed that all three concepts are independently associated with sarcasm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Models and training.</span>
Two CBMs were trained using 10-fold cross-validation: CBM1 with known concepts <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and CBM2 with known concepts <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2},C_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2},C_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. Both models were trained for 30 epochs with learning rate <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> and evaluated using 10-fold cross-validation. A one-hidden-layer MLP was trained with width <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=128</annotation></semantics></math> for 60 epochs. We also trained an SAE with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> for 60 epochs with <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{SAE}}=300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{SAE}}=300</annotation></semantics></math> latent units and a sparsity coefficient <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{SAE}}=10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{SAE}}=10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. All models were trained using the Adam optimiser with a batch size of 512.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation.</span>
We computed task accuracy, Euclidean frustration <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Frust}^{E}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>Frust</mtext><mi>E</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Frust}^{E}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and Fisher frustration <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>Frust</mtext><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Frustration was evaluated on the shared concept subspace <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests were used across folds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Vision experiment (CUB gull–tern classification).</h4>

<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dataset and embeddings.</span>
We used the CUB-200-2011 dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, restricting to gull and tern species (887 images total). Images were embedded using the pretrained CLIP ViT-B/16 encoder, yielding a 512-dimensional representation <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Concept construction.</span>
We selected three visual concepts from the CUB attribute vocabulary: white upperparts (<math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>), gull-like shape (<math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>), and solid wing pattern (<math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>). Within the gull–tern subset, <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are positively correlated, while <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is positively correlated with <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and negatively correlated with <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, forming a frustrating triple. All three concepts were independently associated with the classification task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Models and training.</span>
Two CBMs were trained using 10-fold cross-validation: CBM1 with known concepts <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and CBM2 with known concepts <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2},C_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2},C_{3})</annotation></semantics></math>. Both CBMs were trained for 30 epochs with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> using joint optimisation of concept reconstruction loss (<math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{C}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{C}=1</annotation></semantics></math>) and task loss. The SAE was trained with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math> for 60 epochs with <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{\mathrm{SAE}}=300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>300</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{\mathrm{SAE}}=300</annotation></semantics></math> latent units and a sparsity coefficient <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{SAE}}=10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>SAE</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{SAE}}=10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. A one-hidden-layer MLP was trained with width <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>128</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=128</annotation></semantics></math> for 60 epochs with a learning rate of <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>. All models were trained using the Adam optimiser with a batch size of 512 and evaluation was performed using 10-fold stratified cross-validation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation.</span>
We computed task accuracy, Euclidean frustration <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Frust}^{E}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>Frust</mtext><mi>E</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Frust}^{E}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, and Fisher frustration <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext>Frust</mtext><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Frust}^{F_{A}}(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>, again restricted to the shared concept subspace <math id="S4.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C_{1},C_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C_{1},C_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Paired Wilcoxon signed-rank tests were used across folds.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.8 </span>Code and data availability.</h3>

<div id="S4.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Code and data to reproduce the results of this paper are available at <a href="https://github.com/CRSBanerji/concept-frustration" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/CRSBanerji/concept-frustration</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
